## What still worries me about beating a 19.7k field

1. **The rank is still mostly a TVL order.** 19 of the top 20 by implied value are the top 20 by TVL;
   the multiple varies by category and momentum/tenure move small protocols, but a judge reading only
   the screenshots may call it "TVL with a multiplier". The model is genuinely data-derived (the
   multiple is measured on the snapshot's own 128 live-token protocols, per category) and the page
   says so, but the visible ordering does not dramatise it.
2. **Farm plans are generated, not researched.** They are concrete — capital, hold, cadence, costs,
   times, a before/after split — and vary with TVL, age and category, but two protocols in the same
   category share sentence structure. A judge looking for protocol-specific mechanics (points
   programmes, season rules) will not find them; the snapshot carries none.
3. **No chart.** The snapshot has one point per protocol, so there is no time series to draw; the page
   uses bars and a window score instead. A judge who equates "dashboard" with "chart" may mark it down
   on the page criterion.
4. **Screenshot area.** 1400 × 1400 is ~2× the pixels of the 1440 × 800 the pricing note assumes, so
   each image costs the judge roughly twice the tokens (about 2.6k each instead of 1.5k). It is well
   inside the caps and buys 10 visible rows, but it is not the shape the house priced.
5. **Transcript gate is not verifiable from here.** I wrote every line in this session and did not run
   the kit CLI, so authorship/freshness/usage checks will be whatever the uploaded session log proves.
   If the harness uploads a session that did not quote the kit's anchor, thinking_fresh will skip (a
   note, not a rejection), but thinking_reproduces still needs ≥30% authored lines — the code here is
   all new, so that should hold.
6. **Dossier headroom.** 1.37 MB of the 1.5 MB cap is images; one more screenshot would not fit. Any
   future re-render must keep the three shots in band, or buildDossier refuses (loudly, at least).
7. **Field size.** With 19.7k entries the dev rate cut is 0.960454 and the money band is top 10, so a
   good-but-not-distinctive entry will not pay. The differentiators here are the measured multiple, the
   in-page recomputation and the before/after plans; whether the judge weighs those above a prettier
   page is the open question.
